>> title: Kiriboshi Daikon no Nimono
>> category: Vegetables & Sides
>> tags: japanese, daikon, dried, abura-age, simmered, make ahead, pantry, vegetarian
>> counters: Japanese Home Cooking
>> aka: 切干大根の煮物, kiriboshi daikon, kiriboshi daikon, きりぼしだいこん, 切り干し大根, simmered dried daikon, dried radish strips
>> pairs-with: gohan, mentsuyu
>> servings: 4
>> time: 35 min
>> slack: forgiving — left wet it sours on the third day and you smell it before you taste it; cooked properly dry it will do four
>> step.1: Pantry to plate, and the soaking water is not waste — it is sweeter than the dashi anyone would have used instead, which is why there is no dashi in this one.
>> step.2: soak 15 min, keep the water
>> step.3: fry 2 min
>> step.4: simmer 15 min, until dry

Pantry to plate. The soaking water is not waste and it is not a substitute for dashi either — it is sweeter than dashi, it is what the daikon gave up when it was dried, and it is the reason this file has no dashi row.

Soak @kiriboshi daikon{1 1/2%oz}(40 g; dried shredded daikon) in @warm water{2%cups}(475 mL) ~soak{15%min}, until it has roughly tripled and gone limp. Lift it out, squeeze it gently, and keep the water.

Fry @carrot{1/2}(in matchsticks) and @abura-age{1%sheet}(doused with boiling water to take the frying oil off, then sliced thin) in @toasted sesame oil{2%tsp}(10 mL) in a #saucepan{} ~fry{2%min}.

Add @&(2)soaked daikon and its water{} to @&(~1)carrot and abura-age{} with @soy sauce{2%Tbs}(30 mL), @mirin{1%Tbs}(15 mL), @sake{1%Tbs}(15 mL) and @sugar{2%tsp}, and ~simmer{15%min} uncovered, until the pan is almost dry and what is left is clinging rather than pooling.
